# Binary incompatibilities introduced in 1.9.0, relative to 1.8.1.
#
# MiMa reads every `<previousRelease>.backwards.excludes` file in this directory, so these move
# out of build.sbt and, more usefully, retire by themselves: when previousRelease becomes 1.9.0
# this file simply stops being consulted. Up to 1.8.1 the filters lived in build.sbt and had to be
# deleted by hand after each release, which is the kind of chore that gets skipped.
#
# Every entry here is deliberate and has a release note in the README.

# --- RelaxableVertex deleted, with its companion and the Vertex.createRelaxableWithSet factory.
# It carried the Dijkstra relaxation cost as a `var` on the vertex itself. Commit 9e06526
# (Gryphon 0.2.5) moved that state into the traversal — GraphTraversal.run owns `pred` and
# `bestCost` as locals, and the frontier carries (cost, vertex) tuples — after which nothing in
# src/main referenced it. Only its own tests did.
ProblemFilters.exclude[MissingClassProblem]("com.phasmidsoftware.gryphon.core.RelaxableVertex")
ProblemFilters.exclude[MissingClassProblem]("com.phasmidsoftware.gryphon.core.RelaxableVertex$")
ProblemFilters.exclude[DirectMissingMethodProblem]("com.phasmidsoftware.gryphon.core.Vertex.createRelaxableWithSet(java.lang.Object,cats.kernel.Monoid,scala.math.Ordering)com.phasmidsoftware.gryphon.core.RelaxableVertex")

# --- addEdge moved to the AbstractAdjunctGraph base, where it is written once as
# `unit(vertexMap + edge)` instead of twice as `copy(vertexMap + edge)`. Source compatibility is
# unaffected — each subclass still fixes `EdgeT` to its own edge kind and `G` to itself, so
# `directedGraph.addEdge(e)` still takes a DirectedEdge and still returns a DirectedGraph — but
# the *erased* signature is now (Edge)AbstractAdjunctGraph, declared on the base.
ProblemFilters.exclude[IncompatibleMethTypeProblem]("com.phasmidsoftware.gryphon.adjunct.DirectedGraph.addEdge")
ProblemFilters.exclude[IncompatibleMethTypeProblem]("com.phasmidsoftware.gryphon.adjunct.UndirectedGraph.addEdge")

# --- N and M become order and size: the standard graph-theory terms, and what the Java façade has
# always called them. N and M survive as deprecated aliases with bodies, so calling code still
# compiles; what moves is the *abstract* member each trait requires.
ProblemFilters.exclude[ReversedMissingMethodProblem]("com.phasmidsoftware.gryphon.core.Traversable.order")
ProblemFilters.exclude[DirectMissingMethodProblem]("com.phasmidsoftware.gryphon.core.Traversable.M()Int")

# --- size moves from Traversable down to EdgeTraversable, because counting edges requires knowing
# what an edge is. A VertexMap is a Traversable and does not know: it cannot tell whether an
# adjacency it holds is half of an undirected edge or the whole of a directed one. Requiring the
# count one level too high is precisely what forced VertexMap to carry a mutable `edgeCount`,
# which `+` incremented on the receiver while returning a map whose own count was always 0.
ProblemFilters.exclude[ReversedMissingMethodProblem]("com.phasmidsoftware.gryphon.core.EdgeTraversable.size")
ProblemFilters.exclude[DirectMissingMethodProblem]("com.phasmidsoftware.gryphon.core.VertexMap.M()Int")

# --- AbstractGraph's own M went with it. It was `vertexMap.M`, so it inherited that wrongness;
# both concrete graphs overrode it, which is the only reason nothing user-facing was broken.
ProblemFilters.exclude[DirectMissingMethodProblem]("com.phasmidsoftware.gryphon.core.AbstractGraph.M()Int")

# --- VertexTraversalResult.unit and Connexions.unit are declared as their own concrete types now,
# rather than the base TraversalResult, which is what let `+` stop casting its own result straight
# back down. Same shape as the Graph.unit bug fixed in 1.8.1 — a construction method widened to a
# base type, then narrowed again by hand.
ProblemFilters.exclude[DirectAbstractMethodProblem]("com.phasmidsoftware.gryphon.traverse.AbstractVertexTraversalResult.unit(scala.collection.immutable.Map)com.phasmidsoftware.gryphon.traverse.TraversalResult")

# --- EdgeList gains `Z >: EdgeType`, which retires the cast in its `triplets`. Z was unbounded,
# so the cast was sound only for Z = EdgeType, the one instantiation anyone uses. VertexMap.addEdges
# carries the same bound.
ProblemFilters.exclude[IncompatibleSignatureProblem]("com.phasmidsoftware.gryphon.core.EdgeList.*")
ProblemFilters.exclude[IncompatibleSignatureProblem]("com.phasmidsoftware.gryphon.core.VertexMap.addEdges")

# --- java.GraphTraversal is deleted. It was package-private, had zero references anywhere, and
# carried its own "TODO move this to the attic as it is no longer used".
ProblemFilters.exclude[MissingClassProblem]("com.phasmidsoftware.gryphon.java.GraphTraversal")

# --- bfse is deleted from Traversable, VertexMap, Graph and AbstractGraph. It was declared,
# forwarded, and implemented as `visitor` — it returned its own argument unchanged, so a caller
# got an empty visitor back and would conclude the graph had no edges. Nothing called it anywhere.
# A signature that silently answers wrongly is worse than no signature, so it goes; anyone who was
# relying on it now gets a compile error instead of an empty result.
ProblemFilters.exclude[DirectMissingMethodProblem]("com.phasmidsoftware.gryphon.core.Traversable.bfse*")
ProblemFilters.exclude[DirectMissingMethodProblem]("com.phasmidsoftware.gryphon.core.Graph.bfse*")
ProblemFilters.exclude[DirectMissingMethodProblem]("com.phasmidsoftware.gryphon.core.AbstractGraph.bfse*")
ProblemFilters.exclude[DirectMissingMethodProblem]("com.phasmidsoftware.gryphon.core.VertexMap.bfse*")
